================================================================================
Components used at BUILD or TEST time ONLY (NOT shipped in the runtime binary)
================================================================================

PyTorch / libtorch
  Copyright The PyTorch / Linux Foundation contributors.
  Used ONLY at build time to detect the C++11 ABI flag via CMake
  `find_package(Torch)`. libtorch is NOT linked into the Python extension:
  the `_nuka_ext` extension links only against libnuka and the CUDA runtime
  (cudart), not against libtorch. PyTorch is also supported as an OPTIONAL
  runtime interop target (zero-copy DLPack tensor exchange), but it is not a
  required dependency of the shipped runtime.

JAX
  Copyright The JAX Authors / Google LLC.
  Optional RUNTIME interop target only (zero-copy DLPack tensor exchange).
  Not a required dependency and not vendored.

Eigen
  Copyright the Eigen contributors.
  Licensed under the Mozilla Public License, Version 2.0 (MPL-2.0).
  TEST-ONLY: used solely as a dense linear-algebra oracle in the test suite for
  differential validation. The production sparse solver is self-written; Eigen
  is never present in any production / shipped code path.

GoogleTest
  Copyright Google LLC.
  Licensed under the BSD 3-Clause License.
  TEST-ONLY: the C++ unit/regression test framework.

scikit-build-core
  Copyright the scikit-build contributors.
  Build backend only (used to build the Python package / extension); not shipped
  as a runtime dependency.

nanobind (build backend)
  In addition to being a shipped binding library (above), nanobind's CMake
  integration is used as part of the Python build backend.

================================================================================
Notes
================================================================================

The `.usda` (text USD) scene parser in this project is hand-written using only
the C++ standard library. There is NO OpenUSD / tinyusdz dependency, and no USD
SDK is linked into or shipped with the runtime; therefore no USD attribution is
included here.
